      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is a clear, odorless, and tasteless liquid that is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nTo truly understand water, it helps to look at it from a few different perspectives:\n\n### 1. The Chemical Perspective\nScientifically, water is a chemical compound. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. This means every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   Two atoms of **Hydrogen** (H)\n*   One atom of **Oxygen** (O)\n\nThese atoms are bound together tightly, creating a molecule that has a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a slight negative charge on the oxygen side. This \"polarity\" is what makes water such a great solvent—meaning it is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances, like salt and sugar.\n\n### 2. The Physical Perspective (The Three States)\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** What we normally drink and see in rivers and oceans.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). Interestingly, solid water (ice) is less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils at 100°C (212°F) or evaporates into the air.\n\n### 3. The Ecological Perspective (Earth's Lifeblood)\nWater is the defining feature of our home planet. \n*   **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas (about 96.5% of Earth's water is saltwater).\n*   **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed loop known as the hydrologic cycle. It evaporates from the oceans into the sky, forms clouds, falls back to the earth as rain or snow, and flows through rivers back to the sea. \n\n### 4. The Biological Perspective (Life Support)\nWater is the medium in which the metabolic processes of life take place. \n*   The human body is made up of about **60% water**. \n*   It regulates our internal body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and"
